WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.941
Choosing the model for generation.

2
00:00:02.221 --> 00:00:08.771
Generation is the stage where model choice is easiest to measure and easiest to get wrong by intuition.

3
00:00:09.051 --> 00:00:22.512
The task is reading comprehension over a few thousand tokens, which smaller models often handle well; the failure modes are subtle (a dropped qualifier, a merged pair of facts), which larger models often avoid.

4
00:00:22.792 --> 00:00:26.931
You can’t know which applies to your corpus without running both on your eval set.

5
00:00:27.211 --> 00:00:28.981
So treat it as an experiment.

6
00:00:29.261 --> 00:00:45.347
All current Claude models support citations, which makes a clean comparison possible: same prompt, same passages, and you compare citation verification rate, abstention behavior on unanswerable questions, and answer correctness on your labeled set.

7
00:00:45.627 --> 00:00:57.531
Cost is not linear in model tier at the generation stage either, because input tokens (the passages) dominate: halving the number of passages you send may save more than dropping a tier.

8
00:00:57.811 --> 00:01:08.648
If you later add a support judge, consider a model from a different family than the generator, for the reason Studio 1 gave: errors from the same training are more likely to be shared.
